<div class="head"><h2 id="model-title">What an agentic operating model is</h2><p class="lead">How your company works with AI agents: what they may do, how their work is checked, who owns it, and how it changes when the tools do.</p></div>
<figure class="register">
<div class="scroll-x" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three states of an agentic operating model">
<table class="table table--register table--stack">
<thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="visually-hidden">Question</span></th><th scope="col">Without one</th><th scope="col">One that doesn’t work</th><th scope="col">One that works</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td data-th="">What agents may do</td><td data-th="Without one">Whatever each person set up</td><td data-th="One that doesn’t work">Rules written once, never revisited</td><td data-th="One that works">Permissions agreed, and reviewed as tools change</td></tr>
<tr><td data-th="">How you know the output is right</td><td data-th="Without one">You don’t, until a customer tells you</td><td data-th="One that doesn’t work">Unit tests and a busy reviewer</td><td data-th="One that works">Evaluations and quality gates on every change</td></tr>
<tr><td data-th="">Who owns each workflow</td><td data-th="Without one">Whoever built it</td><td data-th="One that doesn’t work">A name on paper, nobody accountable</td><td data-th="One that works">A named owner, and someone who can take over</td></tr>
